WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.300
- [Narrator] What's not to
love when it comes to lumpia,

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.520
crispy and flaky on the outside,

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.240
juicy and tender on the inside.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.240
It's surely a party in every bite,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.410
whether you enjoy this
popular Filipino finger food

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.250
as a snack or main course,

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.780
lumpia is a total crowd pleaser
at any family gathering.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.760
So from our family to yours,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.480
we're excited to share our
favorite way of making lumpia.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.140
The word lumpia translates to spring roll

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.870
in several languages.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.050
It's evolved for many Asian cultures,

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.103
so there's a variety of ways to make it.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:36.130
Traditional fried lumpia usually
refers to shanghai lumpia

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.780
or lumpiang shanghai.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.550
It's the most popular version.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.870
We also think it's the
easiest one to make.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.060
Lumpiang togue or sometimes
spelled toge or lumpiang gulay

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.130
is fried like lumpiang shanghai,

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:49.670
but the roll is bigger.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:50.900
It's served with white vinegar

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.490
mixed with garlic,
onions, and black pepper.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.690
The filling usually consists of vegetables

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.650
like julienne carrots, bean sprouts,

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.970
sauteed pork, shrimp, or both.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:04.000
Fresh lumpia, also known as
lumpiang sariwa or lumpiang ubod

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.810
is not fried but wrapped
in something like a crepe.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.320
This version is typically
filled with meat,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.500
but can easily be vegetarian
or vegan like this one.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.250
It's commonly served with peanut sauce

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.170
and topped with crushed peanuts.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.010
Now, turon isn't considered a
version of lumpia by everyone.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.880
We think this can pass for
a lumpia, but it's sweet.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.610
A saba banana, which
closely resembles a plantain

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.130
is wrapped in a lumpia wrapper
with jackfruit and sugar,

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.110
then fried.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.750
You probably guess which
version we're making today.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.680
We're sticking with the
classic lumpiang shanghai.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.810
When it comes to selecting a protein

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.780
for our lumpiang shanghai,
we tried pork, chicken, beef,

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.350
and even plant-based meat substitutes.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.200
Some people add shrimp to the pork.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.220
We love this combo, so if
you're into it, go for it.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.490
Just make sure to coarsely
chopped the shrimp.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.600
Today, we're going with ground pork.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.620
Don't worry about cooking
your meat prior to wrapping.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.490
If your meat is pre-cooked,

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.810
it will be harder to wrap
because it won't bind

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.460
with the other ingredients.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.550
The meat will cook during frying.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:09.230
The key to a good filling
are balanced flavors

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.450
and enough vegetables to keep
our protein moist and tender.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.960
In our recipe, we're using carrots,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.370
sweet onions, and water chestnuts.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.840
Water chestnuts are
going to give our lumpia

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.780
a nice crunch and moisture.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.320
You can chop these vegetables on your own,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.960
or simply throw them in a
food processor and pulse.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.430
Just don't puree them.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.220
They should look like this.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.680
Toss this in a bowl with our ground pork

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.680
and add some thinly sliced
green onions, garlic, soy sauce,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.340
Knorr seasoning, sugar,
black pepper, and a large egg

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.080
that's been lightly beaten.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.600
We recommend using a fork
to mix everything together

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.220
to avoid overmixing.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.030
Overmixing our filling
can make the meat tough.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.100
Then cover the bowl in plastic
wrap and pop it in the fridge

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.940
to chill for one hour.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.120
Time to talk, wrappers.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.340
You can make your own lumpia wrappers

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.810
if you have the time,

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.980
but store-bought lumpia wrappers
from your local Filipino

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.530
or Asian market are great
options and more convenience.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.830
Lumpia wrappers are often referred to

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.690
as spring roll wrappers,

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.380
but don't confuse them with
fresh spring roll wrappers.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.030
Fresh spring roll wrappers or rice paper

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.020
are thin like lumpia wrappers,

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.590
but they're clear and
typically not cooked.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:28.810
We recommend using the standard,

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.500
traditional nine inch
round lumpia wrappers.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.700
Be sure to pull all the wrappers apart

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.080
before you start rolling.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.000
This will help speed up
the assembly process.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.040
To keep your wrappers from drying out,

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.160
cover them with a slightly
damp towel or plastic wrap,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.770
so they don't dry out.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.110
If you don't do this,

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.370
the wrappers will fall
apart as you roll them.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.140
The last thing we need
to do is make a slurry.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.050
This acts as a glue and
keeps our lumpia intact

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:58.310
when it's fried.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.200
Egg wash also works.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.400
We did a test with just water,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.880
but found that the wrappers unraveled.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.660
To make the slurry, combine
a bit of flour with water.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.050
And now, we're ready for the fun part.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.550
Place a wrapper on a large work surface

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:16.930
and spooned five tablespoons
of the filling inside.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:19.350
You might think the more
filling the better here,

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:20.760
but it's a delicate balance.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.990
The wrapper and the
filling have to complement

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:24.130
and support each other

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.400
like a pie crust to its apple filling

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.530
and corn tortilla to its pibil.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:29.980
Now, when it comes to rolling,

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.700
it's a lot like rolling
a cigar or a cigarette.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.090
Fold the bottom half of the
wrapper on top of the filling

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.060
and push and tuck the top
half onto the filling.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.640
Roll the filling forward away from you,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.760
then apply the slurry onto
the top edge of the wrapper,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.880
and then roll completely to seal.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:47.713
And we did it.

00:04:47.713 --> 00:04:48.620
It's that simple.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.520
In most Filipino households,

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.960
you'll get an army of people
to help you with this part.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.800
So recruit friends or family
when you're making these,

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.490
we promise they'll love it.

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.360
Once rolling is complete,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.970
we would like to cut
them into smaller pieces.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:07.080
We've found they are
easier to fry this way,

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.280
but you can definitely leave them long.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.120
Either way, they'll taste great.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.200
Now, a lot of families like to freeze them

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.220
to fry later for a party
or a quick dinner or snack.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.690
To do this, set them on a
sheet tray in a single layer,

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:23.200
not touching and freeze for
about two to three hours.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.000
Once frozen, transfer
them to a sealed container

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.650
and you can store them in the freezer

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.510
for three to four weeks max.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:35.610
However, we want to eat them
now, so it's time to fry.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.420
In a heavy bottom pot,

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:40.610
pour canola oil until
it reaches one inch high

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:44.740
and heat until it reaches
375 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.790
Once it's hot enough, gently
place some lumpia in the oil.

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:50.070
Don't overcrowd the pan.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.120
You want to make sure they cook evenly.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.880
Let them fry for about
five to seven minutes.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.470
You know they are ready
when they look like this,

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.820
perfectly golden brown.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.230
Set them on a cooling rack,

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:04.540
lined with paper towels
to absorb any excess oil.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:08.160
Bring the oil back up to
375 degrees Fahrenheit

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.640
to ensure they get just as crispy.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.670
Man, this smells like our
family birthday parties

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:14.950
when I was growing up.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.800
We'll keep doing this until
all our lumpia is fried.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.440
When it comes time to serve,

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.390
lumpia would not be complete
without a dipping sauce.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:26.240
Lumpiang shanghai is often
paired with something

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.550
that is both sweet and
tangy, like banana ketchup,

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.240
sweet chili sauce, or
sweet and sour sauce,

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.020
so pick whatever is your favorite.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.370
And it's time to dig in.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:38.440
Grab one of those flaky rolls of goodness,

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:42.100
generously dip it in your sauce and enjoy.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:44.660
We hope you give this recipe a try

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:46.540
and let us know in the comments

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.300
what your favorite kind of lumpia is.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:53.023
To find this recipe and
many more, visit tasty.co.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:55.695
Oh, yes!

00:06:55.695 --> 00:06:58.362
(upbeat music)

